ernatively, play B8. If someone discards B7, I can form B7, B8, B9. But I don't have B9. \n\nHmm. Maybe the best is to play B5, but no, that's part of the Chow. \n\nWait, the valid actions include Play B5. If I play B5, then I can still have B3 and B4. If someone discards B5, I can't Chi because I need three. But if I keep B5, I can form the Chow. So playing B5 would break the potential for a concealed Chow. Therefore, better to play a different tile. \n\nSo among the safe options, maybe play F4. Since it's the prevailing wind, but unshown has 4 left. If I play F4, maybe someone will take it for Prevalent Wind. But I only have one, so not helpful. \n\nAlternatively, play J3. J3 has 3 left. The player across has J3 in their history. Maybe they have it. \n\nBut I'm not sure. Maybe the best is to play B5, even though it's part of the Chow. Wait, no, because playing B5 would prevent the Chow. So I need to avoid that. \n\nLooking again at the valid actions, the ones not part of the B Chow are W2, W4, W7, W9, T5, T8, B7, B8, F4, J1, J3. \n\nWhich of these is the safest? Let's check the unshown counts again:\n\n- W2:3, W4:3, W7:4, W9:4, T5:3, T8:3, B7:4, B8:3, F4:4, J1:1, J3:3.\n\nSo W7, W9, B7, F4 have higher unshown counts. \n\nIf I play W7, which is 4 left, it's safe. Similarly for W9. \n\nBut I also need to consider if others have melds that might need these tiles. The player across has a Chow of W4-6. So W7 is not part of that. The next player has a Pung of J1. The previous player has nothing. \n\nIf I play W7, maybe someone can use it for a Chow. But since I don't have W6 or W8, it's unlikely. \n\nAlternatively, playing W9. If someone has W7 and W8, they can form a Chow with W9. But I don't have those. \n\nBetween these, maybe W7 is a safer choice. \n\nAlternatively, B7. If someone has B6 and B8, they can form a Chow with B7. But I have B8. If they discard B6, I can Chi. But I don't have B6. \n\nHmm. Maybe the best is to play B5. Wait, but that's part of the B Chow. 